  - [1mServer returns the ID[0m — the user ID is something auth tells you, so it belongs in [38;5;153mlogin[39m's return value, not its
    parameters. This is the shape that survives when the stub becomes a real API call; changing it later means touching
    the signature and every caller again.

  [1mMy recommendation: server returns the ID.[0m "Track who logged in" is knowledge the client doesn't have before
  authentication succeeds — the client only has a typed-in username. Threading a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter in now bakes an
  interface that the real API call will contradict, and parameter shape is the expensive thing to change later; the
  return shape already exists ([38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m) and extends cleanly.

  If you have context I'm missing — an upstream system that already hands this page a user ID — that changes the answer,
  and option 2 becomes correct.
